{{smaller block|{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>There’s nothing that ''allays'' an angry mind
So soon as a sweet beauty.</poem>{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Elder Brother, iii. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|Instead of ''allaying'' the animosity of the two populations, he inflamed it to a height before unknown.{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., vi.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Alas, that neither moon nor snow nor dew
Nor all cold things can purge me wholly through,
Assuage me, nor ''allay'' me, nor appease,
Till supreme sleep shall bring me bloodless ease.</poem>{{right|''Swinburne'', Anactoria.}}}}
'''5.''' To abate, mitigate, or subdue; relieve or alleviate: as, to ''allay'' misery or pain; to ''allay'' the bitterness of affliction.
{{smaller block|<poem>The griefs of private men are soon ''allayed'',
But not of kings.</poem>{{right|''Marlowe'', Edward II., v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet leave me not! I would ''allay'' that grief
Which else might thy young virtue overpower.</poem>{{right|''Beattie'', Minstrel, ii. 32.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' ''Alleviate, Relieve, Mitigate, Assuage, Allay'' (see ''alleviate''), calm, quiet, soothe, compose, still, lull, tranquilize, check, repress, soften, ease, moderate.}}

'''II.†''' ''intrans.'' To abate; subside; grow calm.
{{smaller block|<poem>For raging wind blows up incessant showers,
And when the rage ''allays'', the rain begins.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., i. 4.}}}}

¶
'''allay<sup>1</sup>†''' (a-lā′), ''n.'' [< ''allay''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] That which allays, lightens, or alleviates.
{{smaller block|<poem>You are of a high and choleric complexion,
And you must have ''allays''.</poem>{{right|''Fletcher'', Double Marriage, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|Friendship is the ''allay'' of our sorrow.{{right|''Jer. Taylor.''}}}}

